{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"DOI:10.1016/j.cell.2017.11.009","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":8,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"29245013","source":"MED","pmid":"29245013","pmcid":"PMC5741091","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC5741091"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.cell.2017.11.009","title":"Combination Cancer Therapy Can Confer Benefit via Patient-to-Patient Variability without Drug Additivity or Synergy.","authorString":"Palmer AC, Sorger PK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Palmer AC","lastName":"Palmer","firstName":"Adam C","initials":"AC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5028-7028"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Systems Pharmacology, Program in Therapeutic Science, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02115, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Sorger PK","lastName":"Sorger","firstName":"Peter K","initials":"PK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3364-1838"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Systems Pharmacology, Program in Therapeutic Science, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02115, USA. Electronic address: peter_sorger@hms.harvard.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5028-7028"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3364-1838"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data","recommended_reviews","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"171","journalIssueId":2630217,"dateOfPublication":"2017 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2017,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Cell","title":"Cell","essn":"1097-4172","issn":"0092-8674","isoabbreviation":"Cell","nlmid":"0413066"},"printPublicationDate":"2017-12-01"},"pubYear":"2017","pageInfo":"1678-1691.e13","abstractText":"Combination cancer therapies aim to improve the probability and magnitude of therapeutic responses and reduce the likelihood of acquired resistance in an individual patient. However, drugs are tested in clinical trials on genetically diverse patient populations. We show here that patient-to-patient variability and independent drug action are sufficient to explain the superiority of many FDA-approved drug combinations in the absence of drug synergy or additivity. This is also true for combinations tested in patient-derived tumor xenografts. In a combination exhibiting independent drug action, each patient benefits solely from the drug to which his or her tumor is most sensitive, with no added benefit from other drugs. Even when drug combinations exhibit additivity or synergy in pre-clinical models, patient-to-patient variability and low cross-resistance make independent action the dominant mechanism in clinical populations. This insight represents a different way to interpret trial data and a different way to design combination therapies.","affiliation":"Laboratory of Systems Pharmacology, Program in Therapeutic Science, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02115, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"P50 GM107618","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NHMRC","grantId":"1072965","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH","grantId":"GM107618","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Neoplasms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Antineoplastic Combined Chemotherapy Protocols"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Immunotherapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Drug Delivery Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Neoplasm Transplantation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Drug Interactions"},{"majorTopic_Y